86175635 1145 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/9415dd50c82e3f5f4e47e26f459dda1315f819348e87ff97a4b05b1c27b59891.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,5]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right]$\n\n- $\\phi \\in \\left[\\dfrac{\\pi}2,\\pi\\right]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, \n0 \\leq \\rho \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5}, \\: \n{0} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\pi}, \\: \n \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}2} \\leq \\phi \\leq {\\pi}   \n\\right\\}.\n$$" 13194139534821 true] [17592186175636 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/0437b193a24fadb4e43ec449e685abd9b8a220ff72019d6c5b1d242e6eb49942.png)\n\n \n\nUse spherical polar coordinates to describe the solid enclosed by the hemisphere $x^2+y^2+z^2+4z=0$ for $x \\geq 0,$ shown above." 13194139534821 true] [17592186175636 1100 "Use spherical polar coordinates to describe the solid enclosed by the hemisphere $x^2+y^2+z^2+4z=0$ for $x \\geq 0$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3570/Source/Images/a486e3c62a157d0581ad2221d2947aab.png)" 13194139534821 false] [17592186175636 1145 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$, where\n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n- $[MATH: {\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)]$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents a half-sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,0,-2)$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2+z^2+4z=0\n$$\n\nin spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} + 4z &= 0 \\\\\nρ^{2} + 4ρ\\cos ϕ &= 0 \\\\\nρ(ρ + 4\\cos ϕ) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\rho$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have\n\n$$\n\\rho = -4\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3570/Source/Images/136ebbc722e56fc5a4c38e5789005004.png)\n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-4\\cos\\phi]$\n- $\\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac\\pi 2,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n- $\\phi \\in \\left[\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi\\right]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq \\rho \\leq -4\\cos\\phi, \\: -\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi \\right\\}\n$$" 13194139534821 false] [17592186175636 1145 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents a half-sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,0,-2).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x^2+y^2+z^2+4z=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4z & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ & =0 \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho = -4\\cos\\phi.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/449c0a320c59fc072c7ca38307d9929910b1dfa432d7e3afdf11824e97270dc5.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-4\\cos\\phi]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac\\pi 2,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n\n- $\\phi \\in \\left[\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi\\right]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, \n0 \\leq \\rho \\leq -4\\cos\\phi, \\: \n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: \n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\phi \\leq  \\pi\n\\right\\}.\n$$" 13194139534821 true] [17592186175738 1100 "What is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/a60ddaebcf32cd8d69624b79f34d96d8ca1c18ec59c8bc443a95e235dedfae9e.png)" 13194139534821 true] [17592186175738 1100 "What is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3570/Source/Images/96e952bf87ea43bab876f5dbde7660cd.png)" 13194139534821 false] [17592186175738 1145 "In the picture, we are given a half-cone with the $z$-axis as its axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$, where\n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n- $[MATH: {\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)]$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given half-cone makes an angle of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nradians with the positive $z$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho$ and $\\theta$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is\n\n$$\n\\phi = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$" 13194139534821 false] [17592186175738 1145 "In the picture, we are given a half-cone with the $z$-axis as its axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given half-cone makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho$ and $\\theta$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\phi = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$" 13194139534821 true] [17592186175739 1145 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$, where\n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n- $[MATH: {\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)]$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho=5\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $5$. On the other hand, $\\theta$ and $\\phi$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere of radius $5$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3570/Source/Images/5be9e8b03cf13d47fe1fbf136b253c63.png)" 13194139534821 false] [17592186175739 1145 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho=5\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $5.$ On the other hand, $\\theta$ and $\\phi$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere of radius $5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/4a8c82e617e1d040c61f0f7fca8dd35985005df553aaa53b95e4b14871df6d8b.png)" 13194139534821 true] [17592186175891 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312314 13194139534821 true] [17592186175891 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312321 13194139534821 true] [17592186175891 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312328 13194139534821 true] [17592186175891 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312335 13194139534821 true] [17592186175892 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312342 13194139534821 true] [17592186175892 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312349 13194139534821 true] [17592186175892 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312356 13194139534821 true] [17592186175892 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312363 13194139534821 true] [17592186175892 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312370 13194139534821 true] [17592186175992 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312377 13194139534821 true] 