d8d-322211108f2f" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312407 1034 "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312407 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534821 true] [17592186312407 1111 #uuid "ec5f701a-b33f-5d81-9d89-785021b3dfb4" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312408 1034 "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le 3\\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312408 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534821 true] [17592186312408 1111 #uuid "20d48fd6-16e7-5c3e-abe1-90345a13c952" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312409 1034 "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 8,\\,\\pi /4\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi }" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312409 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534821 true] [17592186312409 1111 #uuid "e261b37f-f10e-5c17-987d-eef37ca2f1da" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312410 1034 "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312410 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534821 true] [17592186312410 1111 #uuid "25c7af3d-00ac-5dc8-9468-b08e260956ba" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312411 1034 "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,3\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312411 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534821 true] [17592186312411 1111 #uuid "aec26e67-0cb9-5ea1-83cf-0ee76f1d7703" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312412 1017 #edb/ref 17592186312413 13194139534821 true] [17592186312412 1089 "q-161844" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312412 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534821 true] [17592186312412 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/3e28d562bb7edd9a067bb3ee2c7cae07dc3b76315f39eeed084bdb06682042a2.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6x,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312412 1113 #uuid "8aaa93cc-15b4-56b8-8ae9-9d4c15dc969c" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312412 1145 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $3$ centered at $(3,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =6x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/70450f3fc9e9971a76e4458c5e020c1f264e739913fbb24d3e6ff22359542316.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\