pi }\n$$" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312417 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534821 true] [17592186312417 1111 #uuid "b1f3ccdb-d337-5232-bbd0-5072139e5a67" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312418 1034 "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi }\n$$" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312418 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534821 true] [17592186312418 1111 #uuid "5af55b41-dafa-5b1f-970d-c1f0b5a064a1" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312419 1017 #edb/ref 17592186312420 13194139534821 true] [17592186312419 1089 "q-161850" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312419 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534821 true] [17592186312419 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/dcd901b032075a4c4a22c991d9bb3e5e5ff49e7a2c91e7e840ede1516085f683.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2y,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312419 1113 #uuid "a7566667-f808-5eb0-a535-838d177964ee" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312419 1145 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/736c46d0121c646def56c6b8fede81a4155fffb97032facd75a6cdf29f98f65a.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312421 13194139534821 true] [17592186312420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312422 13194139534821 true] [17592186312420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312423 13194139534821 true] [17592186312420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312424 13194139534821 true] [17592186312420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312425 13194139534821 true] [17592186312420 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534821 true] [17592186312420 1041 "selection" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312420 1104 #edb/ref 17592186312423 13194139534821 true] [17592186312420 1110 #uuid "2c91a51d-bb41-5a53-a44a-271ae7ad9f96" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312421 1034 "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312421 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534821 true] [17592186312421 1111 #uuid "2d15d3a4-00d0-56e9-b65