y using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ centered at $(-\\frac{3}{2},0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3x=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+3\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/7d161ad5adc8f99f81634049c487f299f84edde255b2f08aa3d1dd4060f99e75.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312441 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312442 13194139534821 true] [17592186312441 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312443 13194139534821 true] [17592186312441 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312444 13194139534821 true] [17592186312441 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312445 13194139534821 true] [17592186312441 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312446 13194139534821 true] [17592186312441 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534821 true] [17592186312441 1041 "selection" 13194139534821 true] [17592186312441 1104 #edb/ref 17592186312443 13194139534821 true] [17592